OK now I understand the code. The `render()` function does `todoList.innerHTML = ''` and then creates new `<li>` elements for each todo. This destroys existing DOM nodes.

The fix is to patch existing DOM nodes instead of replacing them. I need to:

1. Find existing `<li>` elements by `data-id`
2. For existing todos, update the existing `<li>` instead of creating a new one
3. For new todos, create new `<li>` elements
4. Remove `<li>` elements for todos that no longer exist

Let me think about the implementation. The render function needs to:
- Build a map of existing `<li>` elements by data-id
- For each todo in the filtered list, find the existing `<li>` or create a new one
- Update the existing `<li>`'s classes and content
- Remove any `<li>` elements that don't correspond to a todo in the filtered list

Here's my approach:

```javascript
function render() {
 var todos = loadTodos();
    var filtered = getFilteredTodos(todos);

    // Build map of existing DOM nodes by data-id
    var existingNodes = {};
    var existingLis = todoList.querySelectorAll('li');
    for (var i = 0; i < existingLis.length; i++) {
      var li = existingLis[i];
      existingNodes[li.getAttribute('data-id')] = li;
    }

    // Track which data-ids we need
    var neededIds = {};

    // Render each todo
    filtered.forEach(function (todo) {
      var id = String(todo.id);
      neededIds[id] = true;
      var li = existingNodes[id];

      if (li) {
        // Update existing node
        if (todo.completed) {
          li.classList.add('completed');
        } else {
          li.classList.remove('completed');
        }
        // Update toggle checkbox
        var toggle = li.querySelector('.toggle');
        if (toggle) toggle.checked = todo.completed;
        // Update label text
        var label = li.querySelector('.view label');
        if (label) label.textContent = todo.title;
        // Update edit input value
        var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
        if (editInput) editInput.value = todo.title;
      } else {
        // Create new node
        li = document.createElement('li');
